--- machine\system\network
canonical: Machine\System\Network

What this machine's network should be. netd reads the whole subtree on
start and again on every change, and reconciles the kernel to it: links,
addresses and routes on a managed interface are netd's, so an address added
by hand is removed on the next pass. The registry is the truth; there is no
other place the network is configured.

`Profiles\` is the configuration operators write; `Interfaces\` is the
inventory netd writes back (with one operator knob, `Enabled`). Nothing
transient — leases, link state, learned DNS servers — is stored here; ask
`net status` for that.

Default security: inherits the Machine hive root (SYSTEM and Administrators
full, Authenticated Users read).

--- machine\system\network hostname
canonical: Machine\System\Network Hostname
type: REG_SZ
default: (unset)
applies: live

The static hostname. netd calls `sethostname(2)` with it on start and on
change. Absent, the hostname stays whatever it was — or, on a profile with
`Address\AcceptHostname` on, whatever DHCP supplied.

--- machine\system\network controlsecurity
canonical: Machine\System\Network ControlSecurity
type: REG_BINARY
default: Everyone: NETWORK_QUERY; SYSTEM, Administrators: all
applies: live

The netd control object's descriptor, self-relative. Checked against the
peer's token on every request to `/run/netd/control.sock`. Rights:
`NETWORK_QUERY` (0x1) for `net status` and `net wait`; `NETWORK_CONTROL`
(0x2) for `net renew` and `net reconcile`. Absent or malformed, the compiled
default above applies. A capability SID for "may control networking" is the
intended way to let a laptop's user renew without administering the box.

--- machine\system\network\profiles
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles

One subkey per profile. A profile is a *match rule* with the configuration
to apply to whatever it matches; nothing is activated or selected, and the
same profile may claim several interfaces. netd evaluates every profile
against every interface, highest `Priority` first, and the first whose
`Match\` keys all hold wins. A profile with no match keys matches nothing.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name> priority
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name> Priority
type: REG_DWORD
default: 100
applies: live

Higher wins. The shipped `ethernet` profile is 10, so anything an operator
adds at the default outranks it.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name> managed
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name> Managed
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
valid: 0 or 1
applies: live

Off means netd never touches a matched interface: no addresses, no routes,
no link changes, no DHCP. The way to hand an interface to something else.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\match
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Match

What the profile claims. Every value present must match; comparison is
case-insensitive and one `*` is allowed anywhere. Values: `Name` (kernel
name, e.g. `enp0s3`), `MAC` (`52:54:00:*`), `Path` (bus path as in
`Interfaces\`, e.g. `pci-0000:00:03.0`), `Driver` (`virtio_net`), `Type`
(`ether`, `wlan`, `loopback`, `other`).

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address

How the interface gets addresses and routes.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address dhcp4
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address DHCP4
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

Run a DHCPv4 client on the interface once it has carrier. The lease's
address, gateway, classless routes, DNS servers and MTU are applied; the
lease is remembered under `/var/state/netd/leases/` so the next boot asks
for the same address first.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address ipv6
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address IPv6
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

Autoconfigure IPv6: solicit routers, form a stable-privacy address
(RFC 7217) from each advertised prefix, take the advertised default route
and DNS, and ask over stateless DHCPv6 when a router sets M or O. The
kernel's own RA processing is off (`accept_ra = 0`) on every interface
regardless — netd is the only RA listener on Peios — so switching this off
means the interface simply has no global IPv6 unless `Static` provides it.
The stable address is a keyed digest of prefix and interface identity: the
same box on the same network keeps its address across boots, the MAC is
never in it, and the key (`/var/state/netd/secret`) never leaves the
machine.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address ipv6temporary
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address IPv6Temporary
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Also form temporary addresses (RFC 8981) that rotate daily and are
preferred for outgoing connections, for interfaces whose traffic should not
correlate over days. The stable address remains for inbound. Off by
default: a server wants to be recognisable, and the stable address already
hides the hardware.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address static
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address Static
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Static addresses in CIDR form, e.g. `10.0.0.5/24` or `fd00::5/64`, either
family. Combined with a lease if DHCP4 is also on. A malformed entry is
logged and skipped, not fatal.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address gateway
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address Gateway
type: REG_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

Default-route gateway. Overrides one supplied by DHCP.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address gateway6
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address Gateway6
type: REG_SZ
default: (none)
applies: live

IPv6 default-route gateway, usually a router's link-local address.
Overrides what router advertisements say.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address linklocal
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address LinkLocal
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

When DHCP discovery goes unanswered, self-assign a 169.254/16 address so two
machines on a cable with no server can still talk. Discovery continues in
the background and the link-local address is dropped when a lease arrives.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address onleaseexpiry
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address OnLeaseExpiry
type: REG_SZ
default: Drop
valid: Drop or Keep
applies: live

What to do when a lease expires unrenewed. `Drop` removes the address — the
server may already have given it to someone else, and an address conflict
is worse than no address. `Keep` leaves it in place and is what most
people secretly want; it risks exactly that conflict.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address routemetric
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address RouteMetric
type: REG_DWORD
default: 100 for wired, 600 for wireless
applies: live

Metric on routes netd adds for this interface. Lower wins, so the defaults
prefer a cable over WiFi when both are up.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address mtu
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address MTU
type: REG_DWORD
default: (leave alone, or DHCP's)
valid: 68 or more
applies: live

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address sendhostname
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address SendHostname
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

Announce `Network\Hostname` in DHCP requests (option 12), so the server's
DNS can register it.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\address accepthostname
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\Address AcceptHostname
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
applies: live

Adopt a hostname the DHCP server supplies, when `Network\Hostname` is unset.

--- machine\system\network\profiles\<name>\dns
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Profiles\<name>\DNS

What this interface contributes to name resolution. netd merges these with
the lease and hands the result to resolvd over its control socket (the
`subscribe` query); the registry never carries a learned server. Values:

- `Servers` (REG_MULTI_SZ): addresses, either family, tried in this order.
- `SearchDomains` (REG_MULTI_SZ): domains this interface's servers answer
  for; a single-label name is expanded with them.
- `UseFromDHCP` (REG_DWORD, default 1): also take what the network said,
  after the static entries — the DHCPv4 lease's servers and search list,
  the router advertisements' RDNSS and DNSSL, and a stateless DHCPv6
  answer. One switch, not four: they are all the same claim from the same
  network.
- `DNSDefaultRoute` (REG_DWORD): whether this interface's servers take
  names no interface's domain matches. Unset, that follows from having a
  default route; set, it says so either way. Several claimants are ordered
  by `RouteMetric`.
- `DNSExclusive` (REG_DWORD, default 0): while this interface is up no
  other interface's servers are consulted for anything — a VPN's
  "ultimate protection", which also defeats a hostile LAN advertising the
  VPN's own domain to win a tie. DNS only: traffic routing is netd's and
  the firewall's.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces

The inventory: one subkey per interface netd has seen, named by its
interface id — a UUID derived from bus path and MAC, so the same card in
the same slot keeps its key across boots and renames. netd writes `Name`,
`MAC`, `Path`, `Driver`, `Type` and `Profile` (the match winner, or empty)
here for operators to read. Do not configure interfaces here; write a
profile.

--- machine\system\network\interfaces\<ifid> enabled
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Interfaces\<ifid> Enabled
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1
applies: live

The one operator value in the inventory: 0 takes the interface down and
keeps it down regardless of its profile. Persistent, unlike `net`'s
transient controls.

--- machine\system\network\resolver
canonical: Machine\System\Network\Resolver

resolvd's configuration: fallback servers, static names, policy and its
control object. netd does not read it; see resolvd.regman.
